  "query_preview": "..The upper-level weather pattern this morning is \ndefined by a weak trough of low pressure moving into the \nnorthern Baja and a high amplitude trough extending across the \nGreat Basin up into the Northern Rockies. These two features are\nin the process of phasing together as they track east today. The\nweak trough tracking across our neck of the woods today will not\nonly cool afternoon temperatures by a few degrees over Thursday,\nbut we will see localized afternoon breeziness along and near the\nArizona/New Mexico state line. Expect west-northwest winds 12-20\nmph with gusts to 25-30 mph east of Tucson this afternoon.\n\nThis trough is then replaced by a shortwave ridge in its wake \nSaturday resulting in a warm-up in temperatures 2-7 degrees \nabove normal. A surface high pressure building down the front \nrange of the Rockies into west/central Texas late this weekend \ninto early next week will briefly result in a strong pressure \ngradient setting up over Southeast Arizona and Western New \nMexico Saturday afternoon into Sunday. This will result in gusty\neast to southeast winds 15-25 mph with gusts to 30-35 mph to \nthose locations prone to easterly winds. The strongest winds \nwill be across the Gila River Valley, where southeast winds of \n20-30 mph with gusts to 35-40 mph may be possible. These winds \nmay result in areas of blowing dust (especially across the more \ndust-prone areas of the San Simon Valley which impact portions \nof Interstate-10 between Willcox and the AZ/NM state line). In \naddition to the potential for blowing dust, this easterly flow \nwill import just enough mid-level moisture into the eastern \nportions of Southeast Arizona for a slight chance (10-15 \npercent) of mainly dry thunderstorms Sunday. \n\nPlenty of uncertainty next week in the general weather pattern\nas ensemble members offer a wide variance with respect to the \nevolution of a trough of low pressure developing along and west\nof the West Coast. The general orientation of the higher amplitude\nridge to the west of this feature building into the Pacific \nNorthwest should open-up the mean trough, forcing individual \nshortwaves to rapidly pass north of our neck of the woods next\nweek, eventually trying to develop a closed upper-low off the \ncoast of Southern California late next week into next weekend. \nThis would suggest that we would remain dry, with periods of \nbreeziness associated with the passing dry shortwaves. \n\n&",
